236
00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:20,860
it not only speeded up the process,

237
00:19:20,861 --> 00:19:24,760
but actually saved many more hundreds of lives.

238
00:19:28,040 --> 00:19:30,940
While dynamite was used to tame the West,

239
00:19:31,300 --> 00:19:33,420
there were those who were untameable,

240
00:19:33,820 --> 00:19:36,380
and they sought to use it in criminal activity

241
00:19:36,381 --> 00:19:39,120
as immortalized early on in film.

242
00:19:40,120 --> 00:19:43,580
A lot of dynamite was being transported across the frontier.

243
00:19:44,120 --> 00:19:48,200
Because of that, it was very easy for robbers and thieves to go out and steal it.

244
00:19:48,201 --> 00:19:54,940
So then they could go dynamite railroad tracks, stop trains, rob the trains, go into town, attack the local bank.

245
00:19:55,460 --> 00:19:59,160
And the product of choice would be dynamite because of its availability.

246
00:20:02,400 --> 00:20:09,280
But the occasional problems with criminals were far outweighed by the many benefits dynamite offered.

247
00:20:12,460 --> 00:20:17,160
Dynamite played a significant role in mining activities during the gold rush.

248
00:20:17,161 --> 00:20:19,400
and the gold rush returned the favor.

249
00:20:20,040 --> 00:20:22,180
It was the gold rush in the late 1800s

250
00:20:22,181 --> 00:20:29,160
that actually spurred the development of application of dynamite in the mines.

251
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:36,000
The giant powder company built the first dynamite factory in the United States in 1868.

252
00:20:36,780 --> 00:20:40,120
Though destroyed by a blast and later relocated,

253
00:20:40,360 --> 00:20:43,520
it was originally built in the San Francisco Bay Area

254
00:20:43,521 --> 00:20:46,100
where it served the California gold mines.

255
00:20:46,101 --> 00:20:51,280
when water blasting known as hydraulic mining failed to expose the coveted ore.

256
00:20:51,940 --> 00:20:56,860
Dynamite was used to break away rock, revealing rich veins of gold.

257
00:20:59,440 --> 00:21:01,820
Gold may have produced the fever,

258
00:21:02,720 --> 00:21:07,680
but when the fortune seekers cooled down, they stayed and settled the West.

259
00:21:08,680 --> 00:21:13,680
While discoveries of ore deposits of copper, lead, and iron didn't produce fever,

260
00:21:14,460 --> 00:21:18,920
They created jobs and communities and helped build America.

261
00:21:19,660 --> 00:21:23,900
As we say in the mining industry, if you don't grow it, you've got to mine it.

262
00:21:24,120 --> 00:21:26,880
If it's not an agricultural product, it's probably a mining product.

263
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:31,640
We have to mine ore, either underground mines or open pit mines.

264
00:21:32,260 --> 00:21:37,080
And before the turn of the century, after the turn of the century, dynamite was really the explosive of choice.

265
00:21:37,480 --> 00:21:42,320
It was used tremendously all over the United States and throughout the world to develop the mining industry.

266
00:21:43,020 --> 00:21:46,360
Dynamite had a great effect on the population of these areas

267
00:21:46,361 --> 00:21:50,300
because some of these ores could not have been mined economically

268
00:21:50,301 --> 00:21:53,700
by the use of any other type of explosive.

269
00:21:54,980 --> 00:21:57,140
Dynamite replaced gunpowder,

270
00:21:57,480 --> 00:22:00,360
which had been responsible for many deaths in mining,

271
00:22:00,820 --> 00:22:02,260
as it had in railroading.

272
00:22:02,980 --> 00:22:05,300
When these old kinds of explosives were used,

273
00:22:05,420 --> 00:22:08,600
anywhere from 3,000 to 5,000 miners were dying a year.

274
00:22:08,601 --> 00:22:18,020
Well, dynamite is a much safer material than black powder or gunpowder in that it is not ignitable simply by a flame or a spark.

275
00:22:18,820 --> 00:22:26,860
In the days of the early mining, miners used a candle on their cap or they used later on a carbide lamp,

276
00:22:27,400 --> 00:22:31,400
which was just simply an acetylene flame on their cap.

277
00:22:32,040 --> 00:22:38,200
A drop of something burning, an ember, into a keg of black powder was a disaster in those days.

278
00:22:38,600 --> 00:22:45,600
Nonetheless, in its early incarnation, dynamite itself also posed its own dangers.

279
00:22:45,601 --> 00:22:48,600
But over a period of time, it deteriorates.

280
00:22:48,601 --> 00:22:55,600
There's a separation of the nitroglycerin out of the mixture to the surface or the skin of the stick, essentially,

281
00:22:55,601 --> 00:23:03,600
and it could actually puddle up and flood some of these old wooden boxes that may have half a case of dynamite in.

282
00:23:03,601 --> 00:23:07,600
So then it's still very, very, very sensitive, very dangerous.

283
00:23:08,600 --> 00:23:13,100
In cold weather, the nitroglycerin crystallized inside the sticks,

284
00:23:13,440 --> 00:23:17,460
making it sensitive to shock, and accidental explosions occurred.

285
00:23:18,520 --> 00:23:22,740
But over time, Nobel and other scientists altered dynamite's formula

286
00:23:22,741 --> 00:23:25,500
to make it safer under a variety of conditions.

287
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:30,320
Nobel himself developed a gelatin version of dynamite,

288
00:23:30,321 --> 00:23:33,480
which worked in damp conditions and even underwater.

289
00:23:34,320 --> 00:23:38,660
This alternative version of dynamite was used to deepen and improve harbors.

290
00:23:41,480 --> 00:23:44,580
Many companies began to produce the product,

291
00:23:44,800 --> 00:23:48,400
some under license to Alfred Nobel himself and some competitors,

292
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:53,980
and all of these different companies had different ways of manufacturing the product,

293
00:23:53,981 --> 00:23:56,960
different product sizes and configurations,

294
00:23:57,580 --> 00:24:02,740
and there were very few regulations that were able to contend with these variations,

295
00:24:02,741 --> 00:24:14,876
So the industry and the government agreed that it would be beneficial to form an organization to assist in the codification or bringing together of recommendations for

296
00:24:14,877 --> 00:24:16,620
the explosives industry.

297
00:24:17,240 --> 00:24:21,460
And the Institute of Makers of Explosives was founded in 1913.

298
00:24:22,240 --> 00:24:28,920
As the world embraced this new technology, its inventor, Alfred Nobel, grew rich.

299
00:24:29,740 --> 00:24:33,040
But the DuPont family, headed by General Henry DuPont,

300
00:24:33,140 --> 00:24:35,900
was heavily invested in the gunpowder business.

301
00:24:36,660 --> 00:24:39,760
They at first rejected dynamite as the wave of the future.

302
00:24:40,440 --> 00:24:43,480
But his nephew, Lamont DuPont, disagreed

303
00:24:43,481 --> 00:24:48,460
and secretly began to get the DuPont company involved with this new technology.

304
00:24:49,060 --> 00:24:54,200
As soon as the DuPont company saw that dynamite was, in fact, a good product

305
00:24:54,201 --> 00:24:58,500
and it was beginning to make tremendous inroads on their sales of black powder,

306
00:24:58,920 --> 00:25:06,320
They began buying into these independent explosive companies surreptitiously at the beginning,

307
00:25:06,321 --> 00:25:10,160
and then more openly, and by the mid-1880s,

308
00:25:10,300 --> 00:25:15,880
they had already acquired a great stake in almost all of the explosive companies in the United States.

309
00:25:17,740 --> 00:25:22,880
Eventually, the U.S. government grew concerned over DuPont's monopoly position,

310
00:25:22,881 --> 00:25:28,900
and in 1912 broke up the DuPont explosives business into three separate companies.

311
00:25:29,340 --> 00:25:35,560
But while the DuPont suffered a setback, dynamite was just getting started.

312
00:25:39,300 --> 00:25:46,160
Colorado miners used to thaw out frozen dynamite in special ovens where it gently defrosted.

313
00:25:46,580 --> 00:25:48,340
Overwarmed mule dung.

314
00:25:49,560 --> 00:25:52,520
Dynamite will return on modern marvels.

315
00:25:52,880 --> 00:26:06,880
In the 19th century, Alfred Nobel's dual inventions of dynamite and the blasting cap sped up the industrialization of America and the rest of the world.

316
00:26:07,720 --> 00:26:10,900
But in the 20th century, their impact was even greater.

317
00:26:12,340 --> 00:26:19,520
They provided man with an awesome power, the ability to dramatically change the face of the earth.

318
00:26:19,521 --> 00:26:26,580
Projects like the Panama Canal probably couldn't even be attempted if we didn't have something like dynamite.

319
00:26:26,640 --> 00:26:30,280
If you had to rely on labor, it would take just a tremendous amount of time.

320
00:26:31,740 --> 00:26:37,100
Remarkably, a Central American canal that would connect the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans

321
00:26:37,101 --> 00:26:41,320
was first proposed by Spanish colonialists in 1529.

322
00:26:42,500 --> 00:26:48,380
They stood to benefit from the obvious ease of navigation between the two oceans that would have resulted.

323
00:26:48,381 --> 00:26:50,720
But it just wasn't feasible.

324
00:26:52,220 --> 00:26:57,080
400 years later, dynamite made this dream a reality.

325
00:26:58,140 --> 00:27:03,380
At the beginning of the 20th century, construction of the Panama Canal had begun.

326
00:27:05,240 --> 00:27:12,360
There was somewhere in the excess of 67 million pounds of dynamite used in that construction.

327
00:27:13,140 --> 00:27:17,580
And the way it was used was the way we use explosives in road cuts or mining.

328
00:27:17,581 --> 00:27:22,420
They drilled holes, they loaded them with explosives, and they blasted them and they dug the material.

329
00:27:23,800 --> 00:27:27,420
In 1914, the Panama Canal was in operation.

330
00:27:29,780 --> 00:27:37,280
Dynamite had cut through 40 miles of land and excavated 175 million cubic yards of earth,

331
00:27:37,740 --> 00:27:41,800
enough to fill the Empire State Building 500 times.

332
00:27:41,801 --> 00:27:52,500
As the 20th century brought widespread changes in technology, dynamite played an important role in nearly every facet.

333
00:27:53,620 --> 00:27:56,900
In a sense, dynamite put America on wheels.

334
00:27:58,080 --> 00:28:02,900
When Henry Ford's Model Ts began rolling out in the early part of the 20th century,

335
00:28:03,440 --> 00:28:06,960
the assembly line looked like it was where all the action was.

336
00:28:07,600 --> 00:28:10,880
But behind the scenes, dynamite was making it all possible.

337
00:28:11,800 --> 00:28:18,260
As the key tool of the mining industry, dynamite blasted out the iron ore for the steel frames and bodies,

338
00:28:18,980 --> 00:28:23,520
the copper for the electrical system wiring, and the lead for the batteries.

339
00:28:24,980 --> 00:28:29,000
And it was indispensable in creating our nation's highways.

340
00:28:30,220 --> 00:28:35,060
It leveled rough terrain, and it blasted gravel out of rock quarries,

341
00:28:35,760 --> 00:28:38,460
gravel that was used for the roadways.

342
00:28:38,461 --> 00:28:45,360
as americans bought more and more cars they needed fuel for those cars

343
00:28:45,361 --> 00:28:50,320
once again it was dynamite that played a key role behind the scenes

344
00:28:50,321 --> 00:29:01,660
dynamite has a broad history in the oil industry it was used to stimulate wells back in the 1860s

345
00:29:01,661 --> 00:29:10,500
When the wells in Pennsylvania dried up, they dried up because there was a film of wax on the inside of the boreholes.

346
00:29:11,220 --> 00:29:15,860
It was found very quickly that if you put some dynamite or nitroglycerin down in those holes,

347
00:29:16,180 --> 00:29:21,740
you could prekin the flow of oil again, just as it was when the hole was first drilled.

348
00:29:23,220 --> 00:29:27,240
Dynamite has also been used in the area of seismic exploration.

349
00:29:27,241 --> 00:29:36,440
By setting off blasts and measuring the shock waves through the various strata below, geologists could locate oil fields.

350
00:29:37,760 --> 00:29:49,180
As America hit the road, dynamite also was key in building one of America's great tourist destinations, Mount Rushmore.

351
00:29:50,300 --> 00:29:56,820
We know its characteristics very well, and so when we use it in a particular rock, we know how it's going to perform.

352
00:29:57,240 --> 00:30:02,820
And we could actually sculpt a piece of art using dynamite because it's so repeatable.

353
00:30:03,060 --> 00:30:05,300
We can rely on how it's going to behave.

354
00:30:06,480 --> 00:30:11,860
Mount Rushmore was begun in 1927 and completed in 1939.

355
00:30:13,240 --> 00:30:19,020
In the end, the workers had blasted away a half a million tons of solid granite.

356
00:30:19,920 --> 00:30:27,040
As a visionary artist, Gutzon Borblum had used dynamite to recreate nature in the image of man.

357
00:30:27,240 --> 00:30:34,240
During the Depression, Hoover Dam was yet another enormous achievement made possible by Dynamite.

358
00:30:34,241 --> 00:30:45,240
We could blast canyons to shape the surroundings to get a base so the dam could be built,

359
00:30:45,241 --> 00:30:50,240
essentially carve them out so that we could make dams like the Hoover Dam.

360
00:30:50,241 --> 00:30:58,340
But before the dam itself could be constructed, the mighty Colorado River had to be diverted from the dam site.

361
00:30:59,440 --> 00:31:05,680
This monumental task was accomplished by dynamiting the canyon walls, creating four tunnels.

362
00:31:06,320 --> 00:31:14,960
The gigantic 56-foot-high tunnels averaged three-quarters of a mile each and carried the entire volume of the Colorado River.

363
00:31:14,961 --> 00:31:20,140
Hoover Dam was completed in 1935

364
00:31:20,141 --> 00:31:22,480
two years ahead of schedule

365
00:31:22,481 --> 00:31:26,320
thanks in large part to the power of dynamite

366
00:31:26,321 --> 00:31:28,340
it in turn is a power source

367
00:31:28,341 --> 00:31:31,440
the hydroelectric generators of Hoover Dam

368
00:31:31,441 --> 00:31:35,440
supply nearly 1.5 million kilowatts of electricity

369
00:31:35,441 --> 00:31:39,120
to Arizona, Nevada and Southern California

370
00:31:39,121 --> 00:31:45,800
Just as dynamite was used to build the Hoover Dam,

371
00:31:46,600 --> 00:31:49,780
it was also used throughout America and the world

372
00:31:49,781 --> 00:31:53,060
in the construction of tunnels vital to transportation.

373
00:31:54,500 --> 00:31:58,940
In New York City, dynamite was used in carving out the mouths

374
00:31:58,941 --> 00:32:03,260
of the Holland and Midtown tunnels, as well as the New York subways.

375
00:32:04,240 --> 00:32:09,480
The rock under New York is an extremely hard shift-type material,

376
00:32:09,680 --> 00:32:12,800
which is extremely hard to drill, extremely hard to blast.

377
00:32:13,240 --> 00:32:15,420
It required something like dynamite.

378
00:32:15,540 --> 00:32:16,920
Black powder could not have done it.

379
00:32:18,040 --> 00:32:22,300
And yet, despite the enormous legacy created by dynamite,

380
00:32:22,660 --> 00:32:25,000
which in turn was created by Alfred Nobel,

381
00:32:25,600 --> 00:32:27,580
when we hear the name Nobel today,

382
00:32:28,100 --> 00:32:31,000
we don't think of the violence of a dynamite explosion.

383
00:32:31,740 --> 00:32:34,800
We think of peace in the Nobel Prize.

384
00:32:36,060 --> 00:32:41,800
The story behind the prize is as fascinating as the story of dynamite itself.

385
00:32:43,760 --> 00:32:47,760
When Alfred Nobel reached his 60s, his health was failing,

386
00:32:48,260 --> 00:32:51,300
and he began to wonder how the world might remember him.

387
00:32:51,980 --> 00:32:55,840
In a twist of fate, he found his answer in the newspaper.

388
00:32:55,841 --> 00:33:00,840
when his brother Ludwig died in 1888

389
00:33:00,841 --> 00:33:04,520
Nobel got a chance to look into his own future

390
00:33:04,521 --> 00:33:07,620
as he read the newspaper obituary

391
00:33:07,621 --> 00:33:09,520
he had an incredible shock

392
00:33:09,521 --> 00:33:13,820
the editors had confused Ludwig Nobel with Alfred Nobel

393
00:33:13,821 --> 00:33:18,440
and had published the obituary of the inventor of dynamite

394
00:33:18,441 --> 00:33:22,220
it summed up Alfred's life by calling him

395
00:33:22,221 --> 00:33:23,700
the merchant of death

396
00:33:24,640 --> 00:33:28,000
Nobel was bewildered, but he really shouldn't have been.

397
00:33:31,160 --> 00:33:34,540
Nobel's legacy had begun with his father, Emmanuel Nobel,

398
00:33:34,940 --> 00:33:37,280
who invented landmines and water mines.

399
00:33:38,500 --> 00:33:41,280
He went to Russia and was able to sell it to the Russians.

400
00:33:41,840 --> 00:33:45,660
And as a result, he opened up a factory in St. Petersburg

401
00:33:45,661 --> 00:33:49,140
to build both landmines and mines in harbors.

402
00:33:49,620 --> 00:33:51,180
And it was very successful.

403
00:33:51,300 --> 00:33:52,540
The family moved from Sweden.

404
00:33:52,541 --> 00:34:00,280
all the Nobel family worked with the father to develop these devices used extensively by the Russians in the Crimean War.

405
00:34:01,140 --> 00:34:04,400
Alfred himself had also profited from warfare,

406
00:34:04,940 --> 00:34:10,580
for he was the inventor of smokeless powder, technically known as ballastite.

407
00:34:12,360 --> 00:34:15,380
Black powder, when it would be fired, would give off a lot of smoke.

408
00:34:15,960 --> 00:34:19,800
If you were shooting lots of rounds, you'd get a lot of smoke in the area, it'd be very annoying.

409
00:34:20,200 --> 00:34:25,340
Also, your enemy would use that smoke to find out where you were at so they could target you.

410
00:34:25,341 --> 00:34:32,000
And so the use of a smokeless powder so that you could fire ammunition and still have a clear view,

411
00:34:32,140 --> 00:34:35,440
plus the enemy couldn't see you, was a great tactical advantage on the battlefield.

412
00:34:36,520 --> 00:34:43,080
Historical evidence indicates that Nobel's invention of dynamite had only a marginal application in warfare.

413
00:34:44,640 --> 00:34:48,840
Too late for the Napoleonic Wars or the American Civil War.

414
00:34:48,841 --> 00:34:54,980
It was used to a limited extent for submarine mines about the time of the Spanish-American War.

415
00:34:56,100 --> 00:34:58,800
Experts speculate there may have been other applications.

416
00:35:01,600 --> 00:35:08,120
Dynamite in warfare could be used almost in the same way that it's used in the construction and mining and demolition business.

417
00:35:08,480 --> 00:35:12,620
You could use dynamite to dynamite a bridge to stop your enemy from crossing a bridge,

418
00:35:13,100 --> 00:35:15,740
dynamite rails so that the trains would be stopped.

419
00:35:16,560 --> 00:35:22,180
But dynamite on the battlefield could be as dangerous to those using it as to the opposition.

420
00:35:24,780 --> 00:35:28,600
A cartload of dynamite was a disaster waiting to happen.

421
00:35:29,580 --> 00:35:35,400
If an enemy sniper bullet hit a single stick, the shock would set off the entire load.

422
00:35:35,401 --> 00:35:47,120
the military needs to use explosives that are easy to handle and are not subject to shock

423
00:35:47,121 --> 00:35:51,358
accidental detonation from shock in the 1880s the u.s.

424
00:35:51,359 --> 00:35:54,340
military spent several years trying to

425
00:35:54,341 --> 00:36:01,440
create a gun that could launch dynamite in 1889 the navy even built a dynamite gunship

426
00:36:01,441 --> 00:36:03,700
the USS Vesuvius,

427
00:36:04,320 --> 00:36:07,300
and put it into service in the Spanish-American War.

428
00:36:07,880 --> 00:36:10,760
But because of the short range of these guns

429
00:36:10,761 --> 00:36:15,280
and accidental explosions occurring as the shock-sensitive dynamite was launched,

430
00:36:15,780 --> 00:36:18,460
they were far more dangerous to the crews than the enemy,

431
00:36:18,900 --> 00:36:20,260
and the idea was scrapped.

432
00:36:22,840 --> 00:36:25,540
While dynamite had little place in war,

433
00:36:26,060 --> 00:36:28,640
Alfred Nobel was nonetheless going to be remembered

434
00:36:28,641 --> 00:36:31,560
for the bad and not the good he'd accomplished.

435
00:36:32,760 --> 00:36:35,220
Nobel vowed to set the record straight

436
00:36:35,221 --> 00:36:37,040
to clear the Nobel name.

437
00:36:38,120 --> 00:36:40,720
Nobel's will instructed his executors

438
00:36:40,721 --> 00:36:43,280
to use his fortune to create an endowment

439
00:36:43,281 --> 00:36:45,420
with lofty goals.

440
00:36:47,260 --> 00:36:51,660
The Nobel Foundation was a product of his executors.

441
00:36:52,400 --> 00:36:54,340
Nobel left a very simple will

442
00:36:54,341 --> 00:37:08,341
in which he specified that his bequest be used for creating prizes in the fields of physics, chemistry, literature, peace, and medicine.

443
00:37:10,060 --> 00:37:15,360
He gave no directions as to how this money would be put into a foundation.

444
00:37:16,160 --> 00:37:18,900
All that was left up to his executors.

445
00:37:18,901 --> 00:37:22,320
through judicious investment

446
00:37:22,321 --> 00:37:24,680
the endowment has grown to such proportions

447
00:37:24,681 --> 00:37:27,580
that each year the winners are each awarded

448
00:37:27,581 --> 00:37:28,980
about a million dollars

449
00:37:28,981 --> 00:37:31,620
to pursue good work in the various fields

450
00:37:31,621 --> 00:37:32,740
Nobel outlined

451
00:37:32,741 --> 00:37:35,180
but it is the peace prize

452
00:37:35,181 --> 00:37:37,780
that seems to hold the highest honor

453
00:37:37,781 --> 00:37:43,900
the Nobel Peace Prize

454
00:37:43,901 --> 00:37:45,700
has been awarded to only two

455
00:37:45,701 --> 00:37:47,240
American presidents

456
00:37:47,241 --> 00:37:51,720
Teddy Roosevelt in 1906 and Woodrow Wilson in 1919.

457
00:37:52,640 --> 00:37:54,920
Modern marvels will return.

458
00:37:58,400 --> 00:38:01,500
Though dynamite is no longer widely used,

459
00:38:01,960 --> 00:38:05,940
this explosive is nonetheless still utilized at the millennium.

460
00:38:06,760 --> 00:38:11,120
One of its most spectacular performances was after the Gulf War.

461
00:38:11,900 --> 00:38:15,520
Dynamite was used to put out the oil field fires in Kuwait.

462
00:38:15,521 --> 00:38:22,800
When it blew away the oxygen, the fires couldn't breathe, and workers could then come in and safely cap the wells.

463
00:38:25,040 --> 00:38:32,120
Dynamite remains a popular choice for certain jobs in the mining industry and for demolition use in the construction industry.

464
00:39:15,520 --> 00:39:27,500
At New Mexico Tech, a highly specialized scientific environment is used to test new kinds of dynamite, as well as the next generation of explosives.

465
00:39:35,500 --> 00:39:38,680
Three, two, one.

466
00:39:45,520 --> 00:39:55,040
The Energetic Materials Research and Test Center, or EMRTC as we call it, is a research division of New Mexico Tech.

467
00:39:55,720 --> 00:40:03,480
It focuses on doing testing and research in the area of energetic materials, from propellants to explosives, including dynamite.

468
00:40:05,560 --> 00:40:12,880
Today, most dynamite used in the United States comes from the Dyno Nobel plant in Carthage, Missouri.

469
00:40:13,840 --> 00:40:17,360
This is the last remaining dynamite factory in America.

470
00:40:18,340 --> 00:40:22,540
While the factory still produces tens of millions of pounds of dynamite a year,

471
00:40:22,960 --> 00:40:26,860
it represents a tiny fraction of the explosives industry in America.

472
00:40:28,300 --> 00:40:32,280
Dynamite's popularity began to wane during the 1950s,

473
00:40:32,320 --> 00:40:36,340
shortly after the successful introduction of ammonium nitrate explosives,

474
00:40:37,020 --> 00:40:39,500
which had a number of key advantages.

475
00:40:42,880 --> 00:40:54,340
The ammonium nitrate and fuel oil explosive material that is used in mining to a large extent is generally referred to as ANFO, A-N-F-O,

476
00:40:54,980 --> 00:41:03,040
and accounts for probably about a little over 4 billion pounds of product that's consumed in the United States every year.

477
00:41:04,680 --> 00:41:10,860
ANFO costs about 30 cents a pound, while dynamite runs about $2 a pound.

478
00:41:10,861 --> 00:41:14,200
In the mining industry, cost is everything.

479
00:41:14,300 --> 00:41:17,800
We have to produce an ore at the cheapest possible price,

480
00:41:18,040 --> 00:41:22,040
and so the price of the explosives really factors into what you use.

481
00:41:23,540 --> 00:41:26,000
But cost is not the only factor.

482
00:41:26,920 --> 00:41:28,420
AMFO has other benefits.

483
00:41:29,920 --> 00:41:36,480
What's nice about ammonia nitrate, it is not near as sensitive to shock as dynamite.

484
00:41:37,380 --> 00:41:41,080
So it was easier to handle, easier to store, safer to store.

485
00:41:42,620 --> 00:41:46,720
The new generation of explosives come in a variety of forms,

486
00:41:47,300 --> 00:41:49,620
including slurries and emulsions.

487
00:41:50,460 --> 00:41:54,520
They enjoy industry popularity due to their ease of application.

488
00:41:55,360 --> 00:41:58,120
We can pump modern-day explosives through pumps.

489
00:41:58,200 --> 00:42:00,200
So we can use very large trucks.

490
00:42:00,201 --> 00:42:02,560
We bring them out onto a mining location.

491
00:42:03,040 --> 00:42:05,560
We pump the explosive out of the truck, into the boreholes,

492
00:42:05,561 --> 00:42:09,580
can load like 200 kilograms per minute of explosives.

493
00:42:09,880 --> 00:42:11,800
So rather than putting in each individual stick,

494
00:42:11,880 --> 00:42:13,140
we can now pump it into the hull

495
00:42:13,141 --> 00:42:16,300
and do a tremendous amount of work in a very short period of time.

496
00:42:16,301 --> 00:42:17,360
Fire in a hole!

497
00:42:23,800 --> 00:42:26,700
Another reason dynamite is less popular

498
00:42:26,701 --> 00:42:30,140
is that its users have an occupational hazard.

499
00:42:30,780 --> 00:42:33,660
This job is one powerful headache.

500
00:42:34,220 --> 00:42:35,660
Not for the reasons you'd think.

501
00:42:36,240 --> 00:42:40,060
The headaches come from exposure and handling the dynamite itself.

502
00:42:41,000 --> 00:42:43,160
Nitroglycerin dilates the blood vessels.

503
00:42:43,700 --> 00:42:47,620
In the brain, that adds up to pressure and a pounding headache.

504
00:42:48,580 --> 00:42:53,960
As dynamite's use may only last a few more decades, what then is its legacy?

505
00:42:55,420 --> 00:43:02,420
From a scientific standpoint, the greatest legacy that Nobel left us is the blasting cap, actually.

506
00:43:02,421 --> 00:43:05,660
Dynamite was very famous and made a tremendous amount of money,

507
00:43:05,840 --> 00:43:08,140
but the blasting cap is still in use today,

508
00:43:08,240 --> 00:43:12,040
almost in the same way that Nobel designed it over 100 years ago.

509
00:43:12,260 --> 00:43:16,680
Dynamite revolutionized the world, revolutionized the construction and mining industry,

510
00:43:17,060 --> 00:43:19,000
but we've moved on past dynamite.

511
00:43:19,140 --> 00:43:21,340
We still use Alfred Nobel's blasting caps.

512
00:43:22,500 --> 00:43:25,340
But even as the use of dynamite fades,

513
00:43:25,780 --> 00:43:30,180
nothing can diminish the role it played in shaping our civilization.

514
00:43:32,420 --> 00:43:41,920
In looking at the history of dynamite, I think the best thing about it is that it has created a world that we live in today.

515
00:43:42,420 --> 00:43:52,140
It's enabled us to have our tunnels, our roads, our harbors, our metals that we created our infrastructure with,

516
00:43:52,141 --> 00:43:59,960
the metals we created, our automobiles, our railroad trains, our railroad rail, and everything else that we use today.

517
00:43:59,961 --> 00:44:04,100
Without dynamite, we'd have a far different world than we have today.

518
00:44:29,960 --> 00:44:31,880
800-708-1776.

